<setup>

  <selectFromSequence name="A" from="186" to="192" step="2"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="k" from="0.045" to="0.06" step="0.005"/>  
  
  <function name="F">68 + $A*e^(-$k*x)</function>
  <derivative name="FP">$F</derivative>  
  
</setup>

  <p>Let <m>F(t)</m> represent the temperature of a cooling cup of coffee, in degrees Fahrenheit, at time <m>t</m> in minutes.  The following table shows some of the values of <m>A(t)</m> and of <m>A'(t)</m>.</p>
   <!--  <p>Note that <m>a = $a</m> and <m>b = $b</m>. </p> -->

      <tabular bottom="minor" top="minor">
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>t</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><m>0</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><m>6</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><m>12</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><m>18</m></cell> 
          <cell halign="center"><m>24</m></cell>  
          <cell halign="center"><m>30</m></cell>   
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>F(t)</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="F0">$$F(0)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="F6">$$F(6)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="F12">$$F(12)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="F18">$$F(18)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="F24">$$F(24)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="F30">$$F(30)</number></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>F'(t)</m></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="FP0">$$FP(0)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="FP6">$$FP(6)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="FP12">$$FP(12)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="FP18">$$FP(18)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="FP24">$$FP(24)</number></cell>
          <cell halign="center"><number displayDecimals="3" name="FP30">$$FP(30)</number></cell>
        </row>        
      </tabular>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <setup>
        <math simplify name="cd1">($FP12-$FP0)/(12) </math>  
        <math simplify name="cd2">($FP30-$FP18)/(12) </math>  
      </setup>  
      <p>
        Use a central difference to find the best estimate you can for <m>F''(6)</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute name="firstAns">
          <label><m>F''(6) \approx</m></label>
          $cd1
        </answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Similarly, use a central difference to find the best estimate you can for <m>F''(24)</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute name="ans2">
          <label><m>F''(24) \approx</m></label>
          $cd2
        </answer>
      </p>
    </li>  
    <li>
      <p>
        Complete the following sentences to explain the meaning of select data in the table and the second derivative value you found in (a).
      </p>  
      <p>
        After the coffee has been cooling for <m>6</m> minutes, its temperature is 
        <answer><shortDescription>temperature</shortDescription><award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$F6</award></answer> 
        degrees Fahrenheit, and its temperature is changing at an instantaneous rate of approximately 
        <answer><shortDescription>rate of change of temperature</shortDescription><award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$FP6</award></answer> 
        degrees F per minute.  In addition, the rate at which the coffee's temperature is dropping is 
        changing at an approximate rate of 
        <answer><shortDescription>rate of change of rate of change of temperature</shortDescription><award allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>$cd1</award></answer> 
        (degrees F per minute) per minute.  This means that while the coffee's temperature is 
        decreasing, the rate at which the temperature is decreasing is 
        <answer>
          <shortDescription>slowing down, staying same, speeding up</shortDescription>
          <choiceInput inline> 
            <shortDescription>slowing down, staying same, speeding up</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0">?</choice>
            <choice credit = "1">slowing down</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">staying the same</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">speeding up</choice>
          </choiceInput>
        </answer> since <m>F''(6)</m> is 
        <answer>
          <shortDescription>negative, zero, positive</shortDescription>
          <choiceInput inline> 
            <shortDescription>negative, zero, positive</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0">?</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">negative</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">zero</choice>
            <choice credit = "1">positive</choice>
          </choiceInput>
        </answer>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Near the time when <m>t = 24</m>, based on the signs of <m>F'(24)</m> and <m>F''(24)</m>, we expect that the graph of <m>y = F(t)</m> is <answer><choiceInput inline> 
          <shortDescription>decreasing, constant, increasing</shortDescription>
      <choice credit = "0">?</choice>
      <choice credit = "1">decreasing</choice>
      <choice credit = "0">constant</choice>
      <choice credit = "0">increasing</choice></choiceInput></answer>
        and <answer><choiceInput inline> 
          <shortDescription>CCD, linear, CCU</shortDescription>
      <choice credit = "0">?</choice>
      <choice credit = "0">concave down</choice>
      <choice credit = "0">linear</choice>
      <choice credit = "1">concave up</choice>
          </choiceInput></answer>.
      </p>  
    </li>  
  </ol>  
</p>
